Package: mos-ca-trust
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Description: TLS trust anchors for the mos root filesystem
 The whole Debian trust store as data: /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt, the
 individual anchors under /usr/share/ca-certificates, the hash symlinks that
 point at them, and /etc/ca-certificates.conf. Without it the image has the
 code to speak TLS and nothing to decide who to believe, and every outbound
 HTTPS connection -- a podman pull, a curl, an update fetch -- fails with
 'certificate signed by unknown authority' after everything on the device side
 has already worked.
 .
 GENERATED at package build time and shipped as files, rather than pulled in by
 depending on ca-certificates. That package Depends on openssl, which is 2.5 MB
 of CLI nothing on the device uses -- apid's TLS is rustls -- and it brings
 /usr/bin/c_rehash, a Perl script that the package-manager purge's
 dangling-interpreter check rejects once perl is gone. The trust store is
 wanted; its maintainer tooling is not.
 .
 /etc/ca-certificates.conf is ordinary payload and not a conffile. This root
 filesystem is an immutable dm-verity squashfs, so a conffiles entry would
 promise dpkg a three-way merge on upgrade that can never happen.
 .
 There is no Depends. The payload is data with no ELF in it, so there is
 nothing for ${shlibs:Depends} to resolve, and nothing here execs, dlopens or
 socket-activates anything: a reader finds these files by path or does not.
